  <language>en_US</language>
  <keyword>canoncorr</keyword>
  <short_description>Canonical correlation analysis.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>[A, B] = canoncorr(X, Y)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[A, B, r] = canoncorr(X, Y)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[A, B, r, U, V] = canoncorr(X, Y)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[A, B, r, U, V, stats] = canoncorr(X, Y)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <description>
    <p><b>canoncorr</b> computes canonical coefficients for two real data matrices with matching observation rows.</p>
    <p>The vector r contains the sample canonical correlations. U and V contain the centered canonical scores. The stats structure contains Wilks, df1, df2, F, pF, chisq, pChisq, dfe, and p fields.</p>
    <p>If an input matrix is rank deficient, dependent coefficient rows are set to zero.</p>
  </description>
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[X = [1 2 3; 2 1 5; 3 4 4; 4 3 8; 5 7 6; 6 5 9];
Y = [3 4; 1 7; 5 5; 2 11; 9 6; 4 12];
[A, B, r, U, V, stats] = canoncorr(X, Y)]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
